<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trajan's Column</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">113 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s Column is a Roman triumphal column in Trajan's Forum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, dedicated in 113 CE. Its spiral frieze, about 190 metres long, shows the emperor's two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-dacian-wars">Dacian Wars</a> of 101–102 and 105–106 CE in more than 2,500 figures and is a major source for the Roman army and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a>. Trajan's ashes were placed in its base.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trajans-column.jpg" alt="Trajan's Column" width="320" height="746" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trajan%27s_Column_HD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s Column stands in Trajan's Forum, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Roman Forum</a>, and commemorates the emperor's victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-dacian-wars">Dacian Wars</a>. It was probably built under the supervision of the architect Apollodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> at the order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>, and was completed and dedicated on 12 May 113. It was placed at the northern end of the forum, between two libraries, one for Greek and one for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> scrolls, and beside the Basilica Ulpia. The Latin library probably held Trajan's own lost account of the Dacian Wars, the Dacica, which many scholars think the sculpted spiral was meant to echo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The shaft rises from a large pedestal and is hollow: a spiral staircase of 185 steps, lit by 43 window slits, leads to a viewing platform. The frieze winds 23 times round the shaft from base to capital and was an architectural innovation. Its lower half shows the first Dacian war, of 101 to 102, and its upper half the second, of 105 to 106. A personification of Victory writing on a shield separates the two. Repeated scenes show the emperor addressing the army, sacrificing, and the army setting out, while battle scenes are a minority.</p><p>Key episodes include the crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s voyage up the river, the Dacian surrender at the end of the first war, the assault on the Dacian capital and the death of the Dacian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a>. The relief shows Roman and Dacian arms, forts, ships and costume in detail, and Trajan himself appears 58 times. Some scholars suggest the emphasis on orderly soldiers and construction was meant to reassure the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, its main audience, about the army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s death in 117 the Senate voted to place his ashes in a chamber in the base, which is decorated with captured Dacian arms; the ashes of his wife Plotina were set there too, in golden urns that later disappeared. His statue on top vanished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and on 4 December 1587 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-v">Pope Sixtus V</a> crowned the column with a bronze figure of Saint Peter, which remains.</p><p>The design inspired later victory columns, among them those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vendome-column">Colonne Vendôme</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Its inscription, among the best-known examples of Roman square capitals, is the basis of the typeface <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>, designed in 1989. Plaster casts of the relief made in the 19th and 20th centuries, now shown in museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, are in some details more legible than the original.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trajan's Column” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most of the land became the province of Dacia, commemorated on <b>Trajan's Column</b>, and Dacian identity later became central to Romanian national history.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dacia">Kingdom of Dacia</a></li></ul></section>
